steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787841941
      nanos: 177718168
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "not this setion How much could you borrow?\nA quick estimate based on generic rates to get you started. Also add services/slug for each service.  Also restructure the homepage for this: I\'ve analyzed the **content strategy and homepage content architecture** of [Luxe Finance Group](https://luxefinance.com.au/). This is actually a much more useful reference for the redesign discussion because Luxe demonstrates how a mortgage broker can have **substantial content without making the main proposition complicated**.\n\n## The core content strategy of Luxe\n\nLuxe\'s homepage is built around a very clear progression:\n\n> **What do you need? \342\206\222 Here\'s what we do \342\206\222 Here\'s why you can trust us \342\206\222 Let\'s talk.**\n\nThe opening is exceptionally direct:\n\n* \"Our priority is your residential and commercial lending needs\"\n* Two immediate pathways: **Refinance** or **Buy a Property**\n* A visible review rating\n\nIt then moves into three major lending categories\342\200\224Home Loans, Investment Loans, and Refinance\342\200\224before introducing lender credibility and the broader service offering. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\n### Why this works\n\nLuxe doesn\'t try to explain every loan type in its hero. Despite having an enormous service catalogue underneath, it first asks the visitor a simple question:\n\n> **What are you here to do?**\n\nThat\'s a content principle worth taking from Luxe.\n\n---\n\n# What Luxe does better than the current Laxmi site\n\n## 1. It has a much clearer content hierarchy\n\nLuxe offers an enormous number of services\342\200\224residential, commercial, scenarios, industries, and resources\342\200\224but those aren\'t all thrown at the homepage visitor simultaneously. Its primary homepage focuses first on the biggest intents, while the deeper service architecture supports visitors who need something more specific. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\n**This is exactly the distinction we were missing earlier.**\n\nThe website can be content-rich **behind the scenes** without the homepage being content-heavy.\n\n### For the Laxmi redesign\n\nThe intended simple design from the ZIP is compatible with this approach:\n\n* Homepage \342\206\222 broad customer intent\n* Services pages \342\206\222 detailed services\n* Resources \342\206\222 specialist information\n\nDon\'t make the homepage a sitemap.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Luxe uses short explanatory content effectively\n\nLook at the three primary service blocks:\n\n* **Home Loans:** explains the value proposition in one sentence.\n* **Investment Loans:** explains the audience and outcome in one sentence.\n* **Refinance:** explains what the broker does for the client in one sentence.\n\nThe copy is short but specific. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThis is stronger than generic website copy such as:\n\n> \"We offer tailored solutions for your financial journey.\"\n\nLuxe generally tells you **what they will actually help with**.\n\n### Improvement principle\n\nFor the redesigned Laxmi site, every major card or section should pass this test:\n\n> **Can a visitor understand what this is without clicking it?**\n\nOne good sentence is enough. No need for feature lists or extra UI.\n\n---\n\n# Where Luxe\'s content becomes too much\n\nLuxe is a good reference, but I **wouldn\'t copy its entire homepage structure**.\n\n## The FAQ is doing too much\n\nIts homepage contains very long, SEO-oriented FAQ answers covering detailed topics such as self-employed lending, property-buying costs, broker fees, approval timelines, documentation, refinancing, deposits, interest rates, and Perth\'s property market. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThis is excellent for search visibility, but from a **website experience perspective**, it makes the homepage carry the weight of multiple articles.\n\n### Better approach for the redesign\n\nKeep the visual FAQ component from the ZIP, but make it a **gateway**, not a knowledge base:\n\n* 4\342\200\2235 genuinely important questions\n* Concise answers\n* \"View all FAQs\" \342\206\222 dedicated resource page\n\nThe detailed content still exists for SEO and users who need it. It simply doesn\'t dominate the main brand experience.\n\n---\n\n## The \"Our Services\" section duplicates earlier content\n\nLuxe first presents Home Loans, Investment Loans, and Refinance as prominent visual service blocks, then later has another large \"Our Services\" section listing many categories, including those same three again. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThat\'s useful for SEO coverage, but visually it\'s repetitive.\n\n### What to learn\n\n**Don\'t repeat content just because you have it.**\n\nFor the redesign, one service introduction is enough on the homepage. The rest belongs behind a \"View all services\" link.\n\n---\n\n# The strongest Luxe content pattern: progressive disclosure\n\nThis is the main thing I\'d take from Luxe.\n\nThe site contains content for:\n\n* First-home buyers\n* Investors\n* Refinancers\n* Business owners\n* Professionals\n* Self-employed borrowers\n* Commercial clients\n* SMSF structures\n* Expats and non-residents\n\nBut the homepage starts with broad intent and progressively reveals complexity. Its navigation and deeper pages contain the full taxonomy. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\n### For the redesign:\n\n**Level 1 \342\200\224 Homepage**\n\n> What do you want to achieve?\n\n**Level 2 \342\200\224 Service page**\n\n> Here\'s how we help with that.\n\n**Level 3 \342\200\224 Specific content**\n\n> Here\'s information relevant to your exact circumstances.\n\nThat\'s a much better website architecture than putting all three levels on one page.\n\n---\n\n# Luxe\'s \"About\" content is also worth studying\n\nTheir About section is unusually specific. Luxe positions itself as serving clients who need more than a one-size-fits-all approach and identifies audiences such as investors, business owners, high-net-worth clients, family trusts, and Australian expats. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThe important part isn\'t that Laxmi should copy those audiences. It\'s that Luxe **knows who it is talking to**.\n\nThe redesigned Laxmi site should do the same.\n\n### Avoid:\n\n> \"We help everyone find the right loan.\"\n\n### Prefer a clear audience position:\n\nA concise statement of **who Laxmi is particularly good at helping**, based on the real business.\n\nThat makes the brand more memorable without adding a single feature or section.\n\n---\n\n# Comparison: Luxe vs. intended redesign\n\n| Content principle | Luxe                       | ZIP redesign                    | Best final direction                      |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| Hero              | Clear and functional       | More emotional/editorial        | **Editorial + clear intent**              |\n| Services          | Deep, sometimes repetitive | Simple visual pathways          | **Simple entry points + deep pages**      |\n| Information       | Very comprehensive         | Selective                       | **Progressive disclosure**                |\n| FAQs              | SEO-heavy on homepage      | Compact UI                      | **Compact homepage + full FAQ elsewhere** |\n| Credibility       | Reviews + lenders          | Metrics/proof                   | **Real proof, visually restrained**       |\n| Brand voice       | Specific, professional     | Premium but potentially generic | **Specific and premium**                  |\n\n---\n\n## The key content improvement I would make to the ZIP\n\nAfter studying Luxe, I **wouldn\'t add anything substantial to the intended design**.\n\nI\'d change the **content hierarchy inside what already exists**.\n\n### Hero\n\nMake the proposition immediately understandable.\n\n### Service cards\n\nUse them as **intent-based entry points**, with concise specific copy.\n\n### Trust sections\n\nUse real evidence, not generic marketing statements.\n\n### FAQ and articles\n\nTreat them as **previews of a deeper content ecosystem**, not content dumps.\n\n### Navigation\n\nHold the full complexity of the business so the homepage doesn\'t have to.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line\n\n**Luxe Finance proves that a finance website can have a huge amount of content without presenting all of it at once.** Its strongest lesson isn\'t a particular section or feature\342\200\224it\'s the separation between **simple visitor intent on the surface and deep information underneath**. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nFor the Laxmi redesign, I\'d keep the ZIP\'s calm, minimal presentation and use Luxe as the model for **content architecture**: let the homepage introduce and guide, and let the rest of the website contain the complexity.\n\n[1]: https://luxefinance.com.au/ \"Finance & Mortgage Broker in Perth - Luxe Finance Group\""
    items {
      text: "not this setion How much could you borrow?\nA quick estimate based on generic rates to get you started. Also add services/slug for each service.  Also restructure the homepage for this: I\'ve analyzed the **content strategy and homepage content architecture** of [Luxe Finance Group](https://luxefinance.com.au/). This is actually a much more useful reference for the redesign discussion because Luxe demonstrates how a mortgage broker can have **substantial content without making the main proposition complicated**.\n\n## The core content strategy of Luxe\n\nLuxe\'s homepage is built around a very clear progression:\n\n> **What do you need? \342\206\222 Here\'s what we do \342\206\222 Here\'s why you can trust us \342\206\222 Let\'s talk.**\n\nThe opening is exceptionally direct:\n\n* \"Our priority is your residential and commercial lending needs\"\n* Two immediate pathways: **Refinance** or **Buy a Property**\n* A visible review rating\n\nIt then moves into three major lending categories\342\200\224Home Loans, Investment Loans, and Refinance\342\200\224before introducing lender credibility and the broader service offering. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\n### Why this works\n\nLuxe doesn\'t try to explain every loan type in its hero. Despite having an enormous service catalogue underneath, it first asks the visitor a simple question:\n\n> **What are you here to do?**\n\nThat\'s a content principle worth taking from Luxe.\n\n---\n\n# What Luxe does better than the current Laxmi site\n\n## 1. It has a much clearer content hierarchy\n\nLuxe offers an enormous number of services\342\200\224residential, commercial, scenarios, industries, and resources\342\200\224but those aren\'t all thrown at the homepage visitor simultaneously. Its primary homepage focuses first on the biggest intents, while the deeper service architecture supports visitors who need something more specific. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\n**This is exactly the distinction we were missing earlier.**\n\nThe website can be content-rich **behind the scenes** without the homepage being content-heavy.\n\n### For the Laxmi redesign\n\nThe intended simple design from the ZIP is compatible with this approach:\n\n* Homepage \342\206\222 broad customer intent\n* Services pages \342\206\222 detailed services\n* Resources \342\206\222 specialist information\n\nDon\'t make the homepage a sitemap.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Luxe uses short explanatory content effectively\n\nLook at the three primary service blocks:\n\n* **Home Loans:** explains the value proposition in one sentence.\n* **Investment Loans:** explains the audience and outcome in one sentence.\n* **Refinance:** explains what the broker does for the client in one sentence.\n\nThe copy is short but specific. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThis is stronger than generic website copy such as:\n\n> \"We offer tailored solutions for your financial journey.\"\n\nLuxe generally tells you **what they will actually help with**.\n\n### Improvement principle\n\nFor the redesigned Laxmi site, every major card or section should pass this test:\n\n> **Can a visitor understand what this is without clicking it?**\n\nOne good sentence is enough. No need for feature lists or extra UI.\n\n---\n\n# Where Luxe\'s content becomes too much\n\nLuxe is a good reference, but I **wouldn\'t copy its entire homepage structure**.\n\n## The FAQ is doing too much\n\nIts homepage contains very long, SEO-oriented FAQ answers covering detailed topics such as self-employed lending, property-buying costs, broker fees, approval timelines, documentation, refinancing, deposits, interest rates, and Perth\'s property market. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThis is excellent for search visibility, but from a **website experience perspective**, it makes the homepage carry the weight of multiple articles.\n\n### Better approach for the redesign\n\nKeep the visual FAQ component from the ZIP, but make it a **gateway**, not a knowledge base:\n\n* 4\342\200\2235 genuinely important questions\n* Concise answers\n* \"View all FAQs\" \342\206\222 dedicated resource page\n\nThe detailed content still exists for SEO and users who need it. It simply doesn\'t dominate the main brand experience.\n\n---\n\n## The \"Our Services\" section duplicates earlier content\n\nLuxe first presents Home Loans, Investment Loans, and Refinance as prominent visual service blocks, then later has another large \"Our Services\" section listing many categories, including those same three again. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThat\'s useful for SEO coverage, but visually it\'s repetitive.\n\n### What to learn\n\n**Don\'t repeat content just because you have it.**\n\nFor the redesign, one service introduction is enough on the homepage. The rest belongs behind a \"View all services\" link.\n\n---\n\n# The strongest Luxe content pattern: progressive disclosure\n\nThis is the main thing I\'d take from Luxe.\n\nThe site contains content for:\n\n* First-home buyers\n* Investors\n* Refinancers\n* Business owners\n* Professionals\n* Self-employed borrowers\n* Commercial clients\n* SMSF structures\n* Expats and non-residents\n\nBut the homepage starts with broad intent and progressively reveals complexity. Its navigation and deeper pages contain the full taxonomy. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\n### For the redesign:\n\n**Level 1 \342\200\224 Homepage**\n\n> What do you want to achieve?\n\n**Level 2 \342\200\224 Service page**\n\n> Here\'s how we help with that.\n\n**Level 3 \342\200\224 Specific content**\n\n> Here\'s information relevant to your exact circumstances.\n\nThat\'s a much better website architecture than putting all three levels on one page.\n\n---\n\n# Luxe\'s \"About\" content is also worth studying\n\nTheir About section is unusually specific. Luxe positions itself as serving clients who need more than a one-size-fits-all approach and identifies audiences such as investors, business owners, high-net-worth clients, family trusts, and Australian expats. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nThe important part isn\'t that Laxmi should copy those audiences. It\'s that Luxe **knows who it is talking to**.\n\nThe redesigned Laxmi site should do the same.\n\n### Avoid:\n\n> \"We help everyone find the right loan.\"\n\n### Prefer a clear audience position:\n\nA concise statement of **who Laxmi is particularly good at helping**, based on the real business.\n\nThat makes the brand more memorable without adding a single feature or section.\n\n---\n\n# Comparison: Luxe vs. intended redesign\n\n| Content principle | Luxe                       | ZIP redesign                    | Best final direction                      |\n| ----------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| Hero              | Clear and functional       | More emotional/editorial        | **Editorial + clear intent**              |\n| Services          | Deep, sometimes repetitive | Simple visual pathways          | **Simple entry points + deep pages**      |\n| Information       | Very comprehensive         | Selective                       | **Progressive disclosure**                |\n| FAQs              | SEO-heavy on homepage      | Compact UI                      | **Compact homepage + full FAQ elsewhere** |\n| Credibility       | Reviews + lenders          | Metrics/proof                   | **Real proof, visually restrained**       |\n| Brand voice       | Specific, professional     | Premium but potentially generic | **Specific and premium**                  |\n\n---\n\n## The key content improvement I would make to the ZIP\n\nAfter studying Luxe, I **wouldn\'t add anything substantial to the intended design**.\n\nI\'d change the **content hierarchy inside what already exists**.\n\n### Hero\n\nMake the proposition immediately understandable.\n\n### Service cards\n\nUse them as **intent-based entry points**, with concise specific copy.\n\n### Trust sections\n\nUse real evidence, not generic marketing statements.\n\n### FAQ and articles\n\nTreat them as **previews of a deeper content ecosystem**, not content dumps.\n\n### Navigation\n\nHold the full complexity of the business so the homepage doesn\'t have to.\n\n---\n\n## Bottom line\n\n**Luxe Finance proves that a finance website can have a huge amount of content without presenting all of it at once.** Its strongest lesson isn\'t a particular section or feature\342\200\224it\'s the separation between **simple visitor intent on the surface and deep information underneath**. ([luxefinance.com.au][1])\n\nFor the Laxmi redesign, I\'d keep the ZIP\'s calm, minimal presentation and use Luxe as the model for **content architecture**: let the homepage introduce and guide, and let the rest of the website contain the complexity.\n\n[1]: https://luxefinance.com.au/ \"Finance & Mortgage Broker in Perth - Luxe Finance Group\""
    }
  }
}
