  planner_response {
    response: "I have completely restructured the homepage architecture to follow the progressive disclosure strategy you identified from Luxe Finance. The application is compiled and the updates are live!\n\nHere is a summary of the accomplishments:\n- **Intent-Driven Architecture**: The homepage now strictly focuses on the \"What do you need?\" phase of the user journey. The hero explicitly asks the user for their intent, with **Buy a Property** and **Refinance** acting as primary pathways instead of generic \"Explore\" buttons.\n- **Specific Service Gateways**: I updated the *How We Can Help* section to function as a gateway rather than an information dump. Each service block now features one highly specific sentence summarizing the exact value proposition, and links directly to its own deep page slug (e.g., `/services/home-loans`, `/services/refinance`).\n- **Target Audience Clarity**: The *About / Brand Difference* section was rewritten to clearly state exactly who RIVO Lending is for. It now specifically calls out \"Australian families, professionals, and investors,\" framing the business as wealth-building partners rather than generic rate-finders.\n- **Calculator Removed**: The generic estimate calculator has been completely removed from the homepage to prevent visual clutter and maintain the premium, editorial feel of the site.\n- **FAQ Ecosystem**: The FAQ section was retained as a compact UI, but it now acts strictly as a preview module. I added a **View all FAQs \342\206\222** pathway pointing to a dedicated `/resources/faq` page so the homepage doesn\'t have to carry the weight of an entire knowledge base."
    thinking: "**Summarizing Accomplishments**\n\nThe build completed successfully! I\'ve removed the calculator section and revamped the Services layout. Each service now has a unique and user-friendly slug: `/services/home-loans`, `/services/refinance`, `/services/investment-loans`, and `/services/construction-loans`. Everything\'s cleaner and more organized.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I have completely restructured the homepage architecture to follow the progressive disclosure strategy you identified from Luxe Finance. The application is compiled and the updates are live!\n\nHere is a summary of the accomplishments:\n- **Intent-Driven Architecture**: The homepage now strictly focuses on the \"What do you need?\" phase of the user journey. The hero explicitly asks the user for their intent, with **Buy a Property** and **Refinance** acting as primary pathways instead of generic \"Explore\" buttons.\n- **Specific Service Gateways**: I updated the *How We Can Help* section to function as a gateway rather than an information dump. Each service block now features one highly specific sentence summarizing the exact value proposition, and links directly to its own deep page slug (e.g., `/services/home-loans`, `/services/refinance`).\n- **Target Audience Clarity**: The *About / Brand Difference* section was rewritten to clearly state exactly who RIVO Lending is for. It now specifically calls out \"Australian families, professionals, and investors,\" framing the business as wealth-building partners rather than generic rate-finders.\n- **Calculator Removed**: The generic estimate calculator has been completely removed from the homepage to prevent visual clutter and maintain the premium, editorial feel of the site.\n- **FAQ Ecosystem**: The FAQ section was retained as a compact UI, but it now acts strictly as a preview module. I added a **View all FAQs \342\206\222** pathway pointing to a dedicated `/resources/faq` page so the homepage doesn\'t have to carry the weight of an entire knowledge base."
    thinking_duration {
      nanos: 221600601
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1787909904
      nanos: 778788602
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Yes. After going deeper into the actual **Finora source, CSS, asset composition, and animation implementation**, I can give you a much more concrete direction for **what should actually change in Rivo**.\n\nThe important distinction is: **we should not turn Rivo into Finora.** What Finora gives us is a strong *visual construction system*\342\200\224scale, spacing, image layering, section rhythm, and motion. Rivo should inherit those qualities while remaining a much cleaner Australian mortgage brand.\n\n# The core problem with the current Rivo direction\n\nBased on the Rivo work we\'ve been doing, the biggest opportunity isn\'t adding more sections or features. It\'s that Rivo needs more **designed visual composition**.\n\nFinora rarely places content as simply:\n\n> text on left \342\206\222 image on right \342\206\222 next section\n\nInstead, it treats sections as compositions with:\n\n* deliberately oversized type\n* unusually generous whitespace\n* imagery with a physical presence\n* overlapping layers\n* cards that feel embedded into scenes\n* alternating visual density\n* subtle movement so the page doesn\'t feel static\n\n**That is what I would bring into Rivo.**\n\n---\n\n# 1. Rivo needs a stronger sizing system\n\nFinora\'s desktop scale is intentionally large:\n\n| Element                 | Finora implementation | What Rivo should do             |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------: | ------------------------------- |\n| Hero top spacing        |                  85px | ~80\342\200\223110px                       |\n| Hero heading            |           64px / 74px | 60\342\200\22372px, depending on Rivo font |\n| Hero body               |           20px / 30px | 18\342\200\22320px / ~30px                 |\n| Primary section heading |           48px / 56px | 44\342\200\22356px                         |\n| Feature title           |           32px / 42px | 28\342\200\22336px                         |\n| Section spacing         |             120\342\200\223150px | 110\342\200\223160px                       |\n| Large feature padding   |                  50px | 40\342\200\22356px                         |\n\n### Change for Rivo\n\nThe current Rivo implementation should **stop using a uniform website spacing rhythm** where every section has roughly the same padding and every heading is similarly sized.\n\nInstead, create three scales:\n\n### Hero scale\n\nLargest and most spacious.\n\n```text\nHeader\n\342\206\223\n80\342\200\223110px breathing room\nEyebrow\n12px\nHero headline\n24px\nSupporting copy\n32\342\200\22340px\nCTA\n\342\206\223\nLarge visual composition\n```\n\n### Editorial scale\n\nUsed for major story sections.\n\n```text\nSection label\n16\342\200\22320px\nHeading\n20\342\200\22328px\nBody\n40\342\200\22360px\nContent / image\n```\n\n### Utility scale\n\nUsed for service information, FAQs, calculators, etc.\n\nSmaller, tighter, more functional.\n\n**This alone will make Rivo feel significantly more intentional.**\n\n---\n\n# 2. Completely rethink the Rivo hero as a composition\n\nThis is probably the **single biggest Finora lesson**.\n\nFinora\'s hero isn\'t one image. The source literally constructs it in layers:\n\n1. Main text and CTA\n2. A **740 \303\227 413px architectural gradient arch**\n3. A **412 \303\227 495px human image**, absolutely positioned over the arch\n4. A **275px floating information card**\n5. Additional floating visual elements\n6. Decorative elements moving independently\n\nThe image isn\'t beside the content\342\200\224it is part of the environment.\n\n## What Rivo should do\n\nDon\'t copy the purple arch or floating finance dashboard.\n\nInstead, create a **mortgage-specific visual scene**.\n\n### Recommended Rivo hero composition\n\n**Desktop:**\n\n```text\n \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                 SMALL EYEBROW                         \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202         Find the right way home.                      \342\224\202\n \342\224\202          Large confident statement                    \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202            Supporting copy                            \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202        [ Get started ] [ Talk to us ]                 \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202              \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220                    \342\224\202\n \342\224\202              \342\224\202                   \342\224\202                    \342\224\202\n \342\224\202       \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\202   HOME / PERSON   \342\224\202\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220             \342\224\202\n \342\224\202       \342\224\202      \342\224\202     IMAGE         \342\224\202      \342\224\202             \342\224\202\n \342\224\202       \342\224\202      \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230      \342\224\202             \342\224\202\n \342\224\202  subtle mortgage                  small trust          \342\224\202\n \342\224\202  information card                 signal/card          \342\224\202\n \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n### The key difference\n\n**The visual should occupy the lower half of the hero and feel physically integrated into the layout**, rather than being a rectangular stock image sitting in a column.\n\nPossible layers:\n\n* a large Rivo-coloured architectural background shape\n* authentic home/lifestyle photography\n* a small unobtrusive \"We\'ll help you understand your options\" card\n* perhaps a rate/calculator visual only if it genuinely supports the page\n\n### Do not do this\n\n\342\235\214 Three floating cards\n\342\235\214 Fake banking dashboard UI\n\342\235\214 Animated percentage graphs\n\342\235\214 Decorative blobs everywhere\n\342\235\214 A mortgage calculator crammed directly into the hero\n\nThe Finora **composition method** is valuable. Its fintech decoration is not.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Make Rivo\'s images much larger and more dominant\n\nFinora uses images as **section anchors**, not thumbnails.\n\nIts feature cards are a particularly good example. The cards have:\n\n* `50px` horizontal/top padding\n* the image pushed down into the bottom of the card\n* the image spanning essentially the full available width\n* the content occupying the top portion\n\nThat creates this relationship:\n\n> **Read \342\206\222 understand \342\206\222 visually enter the concept**\n\n## Apply this to Rivo\'s lending pathways\n\nInstead of conventional service cards:\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220 \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220 \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 Home Loans \342\224\202 \342\224\202 Refinance  \342\224\202 \342\224\202 Investment \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 icon       \342\224\202 \342\224\202 icon       \342\224\202 \342\224\202 icon       \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230 \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230 \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\nUse **two or three large visual editorial cards**:\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220  \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 First home            \342\224\202  \342\224\202 Refinancing               \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                       \342\224\202  \342\224\202                           \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 Clear copy            \342\224\202  \342\224\202 Clear copy                \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                       \342\224\202  \342\224\202                           \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                       \342\224\202  \342\224\202                           \342\224\202\n\342\224\202      LARGE IMAGE      \342\224\202  \342\224\202       LARGE IMAGE         \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n### Important sizing change\n\nDon\'t force identical cards.\n\nFinora uses a **5 / 7 Bootstrap column relationship**, which creates asymmetry. Rivo should use asymmetry too.\n\nFor example:\n\n* First card: **42%**\n* Second card: **58%**\n\nThen reverse the visual balance in another section.\n\nThat avoids the \"component grid\" feeling.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Add visual rhythm between dense and quiet sections\n\nFinora has a good structural sequence:\n\n**Hero \342\206\222 dark statistics \342\206\222 large soft cards \342\206\222 editorial content \342\206\222 offset cards**\n\nThis prevents visual fatigue.\n\n## For Rivo, I would build this rhythm\n\n### Section 1 \342\200\224 Hero\n\nVery spacious, visually composed.\n\n### Section 2 \342\200\224 Trust strip\n\nA contrasting section, potentially dark or strongly branded.\n\nNot generic \"we offer great rates.\"\n\nUse actual trust-oriented messages such as:\n\n* personalised guidance\n* clear communication\n* support through the process\n\n### Section 3 \342\200\224 Lending pathways\n\nLarge photographic cards with generous padding.\n\n### Section 4 \342\200\224 Quiet editorial section\n\nA lot of white space. Large statement.\n\n> \"A mortgage isn\'t one decision. It\'s a series of decisions.\"\n\nThen explain Rivo\'s approach.\n\n### Section 5 \342\200\224 How Rivo helps\n\nThis can use Finora\'s offset-card composition.\n\n### Section 6 \342\200\224 Human proof\n\nPhotography/testimonial/case-study style content.\n\n### Section 7 \342\200\224 Final CTA\n\nVisually strong but extremely simple.\n\n**The key is alternating visual pressure.** Don\'t make every section a card grid.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Use Finora\'s offset grid\342\200\224but improve it\n\nFinora\'s \"Why Choose Us\" layout is one of its strongest compositional patterns.\n\nIt has:\n\n* a large left editorial column\n* two columns of cards on the right\n* cards intentionally offset vertically by `+70px` and `-70px`\n* substantial shadows but minimal borders\n\nThat creates a page that feels **designed rather than stacked**.\n\n## Rivo adaptation\n\nUse this for something like **\"Why work with Rivo?\"**\n\nLeft:\n\n> Mortgage advice shouldn\'t feel like a sales pitch.\n\nLarge heading, restrained copy, CTA.\n\nRight:\n\nFour concise value cards arranged in an imperfect masonry pattern.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n                         \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\nLEFT EDITORIAL           \342\224\202 Clear advice \342\224\202\nCONTENT                  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n\n                    \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n                    \342\224\202 We listen    \342\224\202\n                    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n\n                         \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n                         \342\224\202 Less stress  \342\224\202\n                         \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n### But change the card styling\n\nFinora uses:\n\n* white cards\n* `10px` radius\n* `40px 30px` padding\n* soft `0 10px 60px rgba(0,0,0,.06)` shadow\n\nFor Rivo, I would make these **less SaaS-like**:\n\n* slightly sharper radius or whatever matches Rivo\'s existing visual language\n* more typography, fewer icons\n* subtle borders instead of obvious shadows\n* one or two cards with photographic/graphic detail\n\nThe **layout offset is what we borrow**, not necessarily the card skin.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Rivo\'s typography should have Finora\'s confidence, not its exact style\n\nFinora\'s CSS makes a very clear distinction:\n\n* Primary font \342\206\222 large editorial headings\n* Secondary font \342\206\222 readable body/interface content\n\nThe hierarchy is aggressive and consistent.\n\n## For Rivo\n\nThe most important implementation change is to establish strict typography roles.\n\n### Display\n\nUsed only for:\n\n* Hero\n* major editorial statements\n* high-impact section headings\n\n### Text/UI font\n\nUsed for:\n\n* navigation\n* paragraphs\n* buttons\n* forms\n* labels\n\nDon\'t let every heading look like a smaller hero heading.\n\nI would also increase the difference between display and body scale. A lot of modern sites look flat because:\n\n```text\nH1: 48px\nH2: 40px\nH3: 32px\nBody: 18px\n```\n\nEverything is too close.\n\nRivo should have **real hierarchy**.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Animation: take the layering principle, not Finora\'s raw values\n\nThis is an important technical finding from the source.\n\nFinora uses:\n\n* `float-bob-y`: **2-second infinite linear animation**\n* `float-bob-x`: **15-second infinite linear animation**\n* a **24-second infinite rotation**\n* AOS reveals at approximately **500\342\200\223650ms**\n\nHowever, the actual keyframes are quite aggressive. The horizontal animation goes approximately:\n\n```css\ntranslateX(-100px) \342\206\222 translateX(-10px) \342\206\222 translateX(-100px)\n```\n\nThat\'s **way too much for Rivo\'s main brand experience** if copied literally.\n\n## Rivo animation system\n\n### A. Entrance animations \342\200\224 primary motion\n\nUse GSAP or Motion for:\n\n* opacity: `0 \342\206\222 1`\n* Y: `20\342\200\22340px \342\206\222 0`\n* duration: `0.6\342\200\2230.9s`\n* stagger: `0.08\342\200\2230.15s`\n\nDifferent elements should not all enter simultaneously.\n\n### B. Ambient motion \342\200\224 only on visual layers\n\nFor hero imagery:\n\n```text\nMain image: almost stationary\nBackground shape: 6\342\200\22312px slow drift\nSmall secondary card: 8\342\200\22315px independent drift\n```\n\nDuration should be roughly **5\342\200\22310 seconds**, with easing rather than Finora\'s linear perpetual movement.\n\n### C. Scroll interaction\n\nUse very subtle:\n\n* image reveal masks\n* parallax of 20\342\200\22340px maximum\n* text fade/translate\n* card lift on entry\n\n**No constant animation on everything.**\n\nThe site should feel alive when you\'re interacting with it, then calm down.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Rivo should replace generic icons with visual anchors\n\nFinora frequently uses icon circles in feature sections.\n\nThat works, but it gives a traditional services website feel.\n\nFor Rivo, I recommend fewer icons and more **meaningful visual anchors**:\n\nInstead of:\n\n> \360\237\217\240 Home Loans\n\nUse:\n\n> A large image of a home/life moment\n> **Buying your first home**\n> Supporting explanation\n\nOr use typography and small graphic marks integrated into the layout.\n\n**Rivo\'s subject matter\342\200\224homes, people, major life decisions\342\200\224is inherently visual. We should use that advantage.**\n\n---\n\n# 9. Forms and CTAs should be physically larger and simpler\n\nFinora\'s hero form treatment uses a **60px control height**, pill-like geometry, and generous horizontal padding.\n\nThat communicates confidence because the interaction isn\'t tiny.\n\n## For Rivo\n\nMake primary interaction elements feel substantial:\n\n* button height: roughly **52\342\200\22360px**\n* clear horizontal padding\n* don\'t overcrowd buttons with icons\n* one primary action per visual area\n\nAvoid having:\n\n> Apply Now | Get a Quote | Calculate | Contact | Learn More\n\nall visible at once.\n\n### Hero\n\nOne primary CTA + one quiet secondary action maximum.\n\n### Service sections\n\nOne contextual CTA.\n\n### Final CTA\n\nOne decisive action.\n\nThis will make the site feel far more premium.\n\n---\n\n# 10. The implementation architecture I would use\n\nDon\'t simply modify every existing section until it resembles Finora. Build a small **Rivo visual system**.\n\n```text\ncomponents/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 layout/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Header\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Footer\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ui/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 SectionLabel\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 SectionHeading\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RivoButton\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ImageReveal\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 ScrollReveal\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 home/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RivoHero\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 TrustBand\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 LendingPathways\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 EditorialStatement\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 WhyRivo\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ProcessSection\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 StorySection\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 FinalCTA\n```\n\nThe important reusable pieces aren\'t generic cards. They\'re:\n\n### `ImageReveal`\n\nControls how photography enters and crops.\n\n### `ScrollReveal`\n\nCreates consistent entrance timing.\n\n### `SectionHeading`\n\nControls eyebrow \342\206\222 heading \342\206\222 body relationships.\n\n### `VisualCard`\n\nAllows the large image-bottom card compositions.\n\nThis will prevent Rivo from becoming a pile of individually styled sections.\n\n---\n\n# My priority order for changing Rivo\n\n## \360\237\224\264 Priority 1 \342\200\224 Rebuild the hero composition\n\nThis will have the biggest perceived impact.\n\n* larger typography\n* more whitespace\n* layered image environment\n* fewer competing UI elements\n* subtle independent motion\n\n## \360\237\224\264 Priority 2 \342\200\224 Rework section sizing and vertical rhythm\n\nMove away from uniform section padding and equally weighted blocks.\n\n## \360\237\237\240 Priority 3 \342\200\224 Turn services into large visual pathways\n\nUse asymmetric cards and dominant imagery instead of small icon grids.\n\n## \360\237\237\240 Priority 4 \342\200\224 Add the offset editorial/card composition\n\nAdapt the Finora \"Why Choose Us\" geometry for Rivo\'s value proposition.\n\n## \360\237\237\241 Priority 5 \342\200\224 Build a proper motion system\n\nEntrance \342\206\222 scroll \342\206\222 ambient layers, instead of adding animations section by section.\n\n## \360\237\237\241 Priority 6 \342\200\224 Simplify CTA hierarchy\n\nFewer actions, physically stronger buttons and clearer decision points.\n\n---\n\n## The design direction in one sentence\n\n**Rivo should take Finora\'s sense of scale, composition, layering and motion\342\200\224but replace its colourful fintech-template aesthetic with a calmer, sharper, more editorial mortgage experience.**\n\nThe biggest mistake would be copying Finora\'s cards, gradients, floating icons and colours. The biggest opportunity is copying **how Finora constructs visual moments** and applying that discipline to Rivo\'s existing brand and mortgage content."
    items {
      text: "Yes. After going deeper into the actual **Finora source, CSS, asset composition, and animation implementation**, I can give you a much more concrete direction for **what should actually change in Rivo**.\n\nThe important distinction is: **we should not turn Rivo into Finora.** What Finora gives us is a strong *visual construction system*\342\200\224scale, spacing, image layering, section rhythm, and motion. Rivo should inherit those qualities while remaining a much cleaner Australian mortgage brand.\n\n# The core problem with the current Rivo direction\n\nBased on the Rivo work we\'ve been doing, the biggest opportunity isn\'t adding more sections or features. It\'s that Rivo needs more **designed visual composition**.\n\nFinora rarely places content as simply:\n\n> text on left \342\206\222 image on right \342\206\222 next section\n\nInstead, it treats sections as compositions with:\n\n* deliberately oversized type\n* unusually generous whitespace\n* imagery with a physical presence\n* overlapping layers\n* cards that feel embedded into scenes\n* alternating visual density\n* subtle movement so the page doesn\'t feel static\n\n**That is what I would bring into Rivo.**\n\n---\n\n# 1. Rivo needs a stronger sizing system\n\nFinora\'s desktop scale is intentionally large:\n\n| Element                 | Finora implementation | What Rivo should do             |\n| ----------------------- | --------------------: | ------------------------------- |\n| Hero top spacing        |                  85px | ~80\342\200\223110px                       |\n| Hero heading            |           64px / 74px | 60\342\200\22372px, depending on Rivo font |\n| Hero body               |           20px / 30px | 18\342\200\22320px / ~30px                 |\n| Primary section heading |           48px / 56px | 44\342\200\22356px                         |\n| Feature title           |           32px / 42px | 28\342\200\22336px                         |\n| Section spacing         |             120\342\200\223150px | 110\342\200\223160px                       |\n| Large feature padding   |                  50px | 40\342\200\22356px                         |\n\n### Change for Rivo\n\nThe current Rivo implementation should **stop using a uniform website spacing rhythm** where every section has roughly the same padding and every heading is similarly sized.\n\nInstead, create three scales:\n\n### Hero scale\n\nLargest and most spacious.\n\n```text\nHeader\n\342\206\223\n80\342\200\223110px breathing room\nEyebrow\n12px\nHero headline\n24px\nSupporting copy\n32\342\200\22340px\nCTA\n\342\206\223\nLarge visual composition\n```\n\n### Editorial scale\n\nUsed for major story sections.\n\n```text\nSection label\n16\342\200\22320px\nHeading\n20\342\200\22328px\nBody\n40\342\200\22360px\nContent / image\n```\n\n### Utility scale\n\nUsed for service information, FAQs, calculators, etc.\n\nSmaller, tighter, more functional.\n\n**This alone will make Rivo feel significantly more intentional.**\n\n---\n\n# 2. Completely rethink the Rivo hero as a composition\n\nThis is probably the **single biggest Finora lesson**.\n\nFinora\'s hero isn\'t one image. The source literally constructs it in layers:\n\n1. Main text and CTA\n2. A **740 \303\227 413px architectural gradient arch**\n3. A **412 \303\227 495px human image**, absolutely positioned over the arch\n4. A **275px floating information card**\n5. Additional floating visual elements\n6. Decorative elements moving independently\n\nThe image isn\'t beside the content\342\200\224it is part of the environment.\n\n## What Rivo should do\n\nDon\'t copy the purple arch or floating finance dashboard.\n\nInstead, create a **mortgage-specific visual scene**.\n\n### Recommended Rivo hero composition\n\n**Desktop:**\n\n```text\n \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                 SMALL EYEBROW                         \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202         Find the right way home.                      \342\224\202\n \342\224\202          Large confident statement                    \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202            Supporting copy                            \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202        [ Get started ] [ Talk to us ]                 \342\224\202\n \342\224\202                                                       \342\224\202\n \342\224\202              \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220                    \342\224\202\n \342\224\202              \342\224\202                   \342\224\202                    \342\224\202\n \342\224\202       \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\202   HOME / PERSON   \342\224\202\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220             \342\224\202\n \342\224\202       \342\224\202      \342\224\202     IMAGE         \342\224\202      \342\224\202             \342\224\202\n \342\224\202       \342\224\202      \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230      \342\224\202             \342\224\202\n \342\224\202  subtle mortgage                  small trust          \342\224\202\n \342\224\202  information card                 signal/card          \342\224\202\n \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n### The key difference\n\n**The visual should occupy the lower half of the hero and feel physically integrated into the layout**, rather than being a rectangular stock image sitting in a column.\n\nPossible layers:\n\n* a large Rivo-coloured architectural background shape\n* authentic home/lifestyle photography\n* a small unobtrusive \"We\'ll help you understand your options\" card\n* perhaps a rate/calculator visual only if it genuinely supports the page\n\n### Do not do this\n\n\342\235\214 Three floating cards\n\342\235\214 Fake banking dashboard UI\n\342\235\214 Animated percentage graphs\n\342\235\214 Decorative blobs everywhere\n\342\235\214 A mortgage calculator crammed directly into the hero\n\nThe Finora **composition method** is valuable. Its fintech decoration is not.\n\n---\n\n# 3. Make Rivo\'s images much larger and more dominant\n\nFinora uses images as **section anchors**, not thumbnails.\n\nIts feature cards are a particularly good example. The cards have:\n\n* `50px` horizontal/top padding\n* the image pushed down into the bottom of the card\n* the image spanning essentially the full available width\n* the content occupying the top portion\n\nThat creates this relationship:\n\n> **Read \342\206\222 understand \342\206\222 visually enter the concept**\n\n## Apply this to Rivo\'s lending pathways\n\nInstead of conventional service cards:\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220 \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220 \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 Home Loans \342\224\202 \342\224\202 Refinance  \342\224\202 \342\224\202 Investment \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 icon       \342\224\202 \342\224\202 icon       \342\224\202 \342\224\202 icon       \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230 \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230 \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\nUse **two or three large visual editorial cards**:\n\n```text\n\342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220  \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n\342\224\202 First home            \342\224\202  \342\224\202 Refinancing               \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                       \342\224\202  \342\224\202                           \342\224\202\n\342\224\202 Clear copy            \342\224\202  \342\224\202 Clear copy                \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                       \342\224\202  \342\224\202                           \342\224\202\n\342\224\202                       \342\224\202  \342\224\202                           \342\224\202\n\342\224\202      LARGE IMAGE      \342\224\202  \342\224\202       LARGE IMAGE         \342\224\202\n\342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n### Important sizing change\n\nDon\'t force identical cards.\n\nFinora uses a **5 / 7 Bootstrap column relationship**, which creates asymmetry. Rivo should use asymmetry too.\n\nFor example:\n\n* First card: **42%**\n* Second card: **58%**\n\nThen reverse the visual balance in another section.\n\nThat avoids the \"component grid\" feeling.\n\n---\n\n# 4. Add visual rhythm between dense and quiet sections\n\nFinora has a good structural sequence:\n\n**Hero \342\206\222 dark statistics \342\206\222 large soft cards \342\206\222 editorial content \342\206\222 offset cards**\n\nThis prevents visual fatigue.\n\n## For Rivo, I would build this rhythm\n\n### Section 1 \342\200\224 Hero\n\nVery spacious, visually composed.\n\n### Section 2 \342\200\224 Trust strip\n\nA contrasting section, potentially dark or strongly branded.\n\nNot generic \"we offer great rates.\"\n\nUse actual trust-oriented messages such as:\n\n* personalised guidance\n* clear communication\n* support through the process\n\n### Section 3 \342\200\224 Lending pathways\n\nLarge photographic cards with generous padding.\n\n### Section 4 \342\200\224 Quiet editorial section\n\nA lot of white space. Large statement.\n\n> \"A mortgage isn\'t one decision. It\'s a series of decisions.\"\n\nThen explain Rivo\'s approach.\n\n### Section 5 \342\200\224 How Rivo helps\n\nThis can use Finora\'s offset-card composition.\n\n### Section 6 \342\200\224 Human proof\n\nPhotography/testimonial/case-study style content.\n\n### Section 7 \342\200\224 Final CTA\n\nVisually strong but extremely simple.\n\n**The key is alternating visual pressure.** Don\'t make every section a card grid.\n\n---\n\n# 5. Use Finora\'s offset grid\342\200\224but improve it\n\nFinora\'s \"Why Choose Us\" layout is one of its strongest compositional patterns.\n\nIt has:\n\n* a large left editorial column\n* two columns of cards on the right\n* cards intentionally offset vertically by `+70px` and `-70px`\n* substantial shadows but minimal borders\n\nThat creates a page that feels **designed rather than stacked**.\n\n## Rivo adaptation\n\nUse this for something like **\"Why work with Rivo?\"**\n\nLeft:\n\n> Mortgage advice shouldn\'t feel like a sales pitch.\n\nLarge heading, restrained copy, CTA.\n\nRight:\n\nFour concise value cards arranged in an imperfect masonry pattern.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n                         \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\nLEFT EDITORIAL           \342\224\202 Clear advice \342\224\202\nCONTENT                  \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n\n                    \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n                    \342\224\202 We listen    \342\224\202\n                    \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n\n                         \342\224\214\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\220\n                         \342\224\202 Less stress  \342\224\202\n                         \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\200\342\224\230\n```\n\n### But change the card styling\n\nFinora uses:\n\n* white cards\n* `10px` radius\n* `40px 30px` padding\n* soft `0 10px 60px rgba(0,0,0,.06)` shadow\n\nFor Rivo, I would make these **less SaaS-like**:\n\n* slightly sharper radius or whatever matches Rivo\'s existing visual language\n* more typography, fewer icons\n* subtle borders instead of obvious shadows\n* one or two cards with photographic/graphic detail\n\nThe **layout offset is what we borrow**, not necessarily the card skin.\n\n---\n\n# 6. Rivo\'s typography should have Finora\'s confidence, not its exact style\n\nFinora\'s CSS makes a very clear distinction:\n\n* Primary font \342\206\222 large editorial headings\n* Secondary font \342\206\222 readable body/interface content\n\nThe hierarchy is aggressive and consistent.\n\n## For Rivo\n\nThe most important implementation change is to establish strict typography roles.\n\n### Display\n\nUsed only for:\n\n* Hero\n* major editorial statements\n* high-impact section headings\n\n### Text/UI font\n\nUsed for:\n\n* navigation\n* paragraphs\n* buttons\n* forms\n* labels\n\nDon\'t let every heading look like a smaller hero heading.\n\nI would also increase the difference between display and body scale. A lot of modern sites look flat because:\n\n```text\nH1: 48px\nH2: 40px\nH3: 32px\nBody: 18px\n```\n\nEverything is too close.\n\nRivo should have **real hierarchy**.\n\n---\n\n# 7. Animation: take the layering principle, not Finora\'s raw values\n\nThis is an important technical finding from the source.\n\nFinora uses:\n\n* `float-bob-y`: **2-second infinite linear animation**\n* `float-bob-x`: **15-second infinite linear animation**\n* a **24-second infinite rotation**\n* AOS reveals at approximately **500\342\200\223650ms**\n\nHowever, the actual keyframes are quite aggressive. The horizontal animation goes approximately:\n\n```css\ntranslateX(-100px) \342\206\222 translateX(-10px) \342\206\222 translateX(-100px)\n```\n\nThat\'s **way too much for Rivo\'s main brand experience** if copied literally.\n\n## Rivo animation system\n\n### A. Entrance animations \342\200\224 primary motion\n\nUse GSAP or Motion for:\n\n* opacity: `0 \342\206\222 1`\n* Y: `20\342\200\22340px \342\206\222 0`\n* duration: `0.6\342\200\2230.9s`\n* stagger: `0.08\342\200\2230.15s`\n\nDifferent elements should not all enter simultaneously.\n\n### B. Ambient motion \342\200\224 only on visual layers\n\nFor hero imagery:\n\n```text\nMain image: almost stationary\nBackground shape: 6\342\200\22312px slow drift\nSmall secondary card: 8\342\200\22315px independent drift\n```\n\nDuration should be roughly **5\342\200\22310 seconds**, with easing rather than Finora\'s linear perpetual movement.\n\n### C. Scroll interaction\n\nUse very subtle:\n\n* image reveal masks\n* parallax of 20\342\200\22340px maximum\n* text fade/translate\n* card lift on entry\n\n**No constant animation on everything.**\n\nThe site should feel alive when you\'re interacting with it, then calm down.\n\n---\n\n# 8. Rivo should replace generic icons with visual anchors\n\nFinora frequently uses icon circles in feature sections.\n\nThat works, but it gives a traditional services website feel.\n\nFor Rivo, I recommend fewer icons and more **meaningful visual anchors**:\n\nInstead of:\n\n> \360\237\217\240 Home Loans\n\nUse:\n\n> A large image of a home/life moment\n> **Buying your first home**\n> Supporting explanation\n\nOr use typography and small graphic marks integrated into the layout.\n\n**Rivo\'s subject matter\342\200\224homes, people, major life decisions\342\200\224is inherently visual. We should use that advantage.**\n\n---\n\n# 9. Forms and CTAs should be physically larger and simpler\n\nFinora\'s hero form treatment uses a **60px control height**, pill-like geometry, and generous horizontal padding.\n\nThat communicates confidence because the interaction isn\'t tiny.\n\n## For Rivo\n\nMake primary interaction elements feel substantial:\n\n* button height: roughly **52\342\200\22360px**\n* clear horizontal padding\n* don\'t overcrowd buttons with icons\n* one primary action per visual area\n\nAvoid having:\n\n> Apply Now | Get a Quote | Calculate | Contact | Learn More\n\nall visible at once.\n\n### Hero\n\nOne primary CTA + one quiet secondary action maximum.\n\n### Service sections\n\nOne contextual CTA.\n\n### Final CTA\n\nOne decisive action.\n\nThis will make the site feel far more premium.\n\n---\n\n# 10. The implementation architecture I would use\n\nDon\'t simply modify every existing section until it resembles Finora. Build a small **Rivo visual system**.\n\n```text\ncomponents/\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 layout/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 Header\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 Footer\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ui/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 SectionLabel\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 SectionHeading\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RivoButton\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ImageReveal\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 ScrollReveal\n\342\224\202\n\342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 home/\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 RivoHero\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 TrustBand\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 LendingPathways\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 EditorialStatement\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 WhyRivo\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 ProcessSection\n\342\224\202   \342\224\234\342\224\200\342\224\200 StorySection\n\342\224\202   \342\224\224\342\224\200\342\224\200 FinalCTA\n```\n\nThe important reusable pieces aren\'t generic cards. They\'re:\n\n### `ImageReveal`\n\nControls how photography enters and crops.\n\n### `ScrollReveal`\n\nCreates consistent entrance timing.\n\n### `SectionHeading`\n\nControls eyebrow \342\206\222 heading \342\206\222 body relationships.\n\n### `VisualCard`\n\nAllows the large image-bottom card compositions.\n\nThis will prevent Rivo from becoming a pile of individually styled sections.\n\n---\n\n# My priority order for changing Rivo\n\n## \360\237\224\264 Priority 1 \342\200\224 Rebuild the hero composition\n\nThis will have the biggest perceived impact.\n\n* larger typography\n* more whitespace\n* layered image environment\n* fewer competing UI elements\n* subtle independent motion\n\n## \360\237\224\264 Priority 2 \342\200\224 Rework section sizing and vertical rhythm\n\nMove away from uniform section padding and equally weighted blocks.\n\n## \360\237\237\240 Priority 3 \342\200\224 Turn services into large visual pathways\n\nUse asymmetric cards and dominant imagery instead of small icon grids.\n\n## \360\237\237\240 Priority 4 \342\200\224 Add the offset editorial/card composition\n\nAdapt the Finora \"Why Choose Us\" geometry for Rivo\'s value proposition.\n\n## \360\237\237\241 Priority 5 \342\200\224 Build a proper motion system\n\nEntrance \342\206\222 scroll \342\206\222 ambient layers, instead of adding animations section by section.\n\n## \360\237\237\241 Priority 6 \342\200\224 Simplify CTA hierarchy\n\nFewer actions, physically stronger buttons and clearer decision points.\n\n---\n\n## The design direction in one sentence\n\n**Rivo should take Finora\'s sense of scale, composition, layering and motion\342\200\224but replace its colourful fintech-template aesthetic with a calmer, sharper, more editorial mortgage experience.**\n\nThe biggest mistake would be copying Finora\'s cards, gradients, floating icons and colours. The biggest opportunity is copying **how Finora constructs visual moments** and applying that discipline to Rivo\'s existing brand and mortgage content."
    }
  }
}
