    thinking: "**Investigating Unnecessary Bundling**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that `whatwg-fetch` resides within `firebase-tools`, which is solely a devDependency. My concern was the potential unnecessary bundling of it for the client. I\'ve double-checked my imports with grep and can confirm I haven\'t directly imported anything from `firebase-tools`, or packages like `firebase/auth`. This narrows down the possibilities of where it might be being bundled from.\n\n\n**Analyzing Fetch Conflicts**\n\nOkay, I\'m now exploring the Next.js side of this `fetch` issue. It seems the Dev Overlay or App Router in Next.js (version 13+) can patch `window.fetch`. This could be the source of the read-only error. I\'m considering potential solutions, like a script in `app/layout.tsx` to alter the fetch property. However, if the property is not configurable, I am stopped. I am also investigating the impact of the iframe sandbox environment. The latest user feedback provides a new error.\n\n\n**Verifying AI Studio Compilation**\n\nI realize I skipped a crucial step: calling `compile_applet`. I only ran `npm run build`, which completed without errors. However, `compile_applet` is specifically designed to check and confirm AI Studio applet builds, and I neglected that. The user encountered an unexpected error, possibly linked to this oversight, though I\'m also considering the possibility of a platform error that is not related to my code. The metadata error is relevant and seems to indicate an issue with `fetch`.\n\n\n**Addressing Fetch Property**\n\nI\'ve got a clearer picture now. The error, \"Cannot set property fetch of #<Window> which has only a getter,\" points directly to Next.js HMR or a Next.js script trying to overwrite the browser\'s `fetch`, but `window.fetch` is a getter because the AI Studio environment wraps it. I\'m thinking I can fix this. I\'m going to add a script to `layout.tsx` that will run before Next.js and redefine `window.fetch` so Next.js can then wrap it. I hope this works.\n\n\n"
