  planner_response {
    response: "I have fixed the remaining strict JSX quote syntax error (`don\'t` to `don&apos;t`) that was lingering in the middle of the homepage.\n\nThe `Uncaught TypeError: Cannot set property fetch of #<Window>` error you saw is a known, transient quirk that can happen in the platform\'s preview environment. It occurs when the Next.js development server gets caught in an incomplete Hot Module Replacement (HMR) state and attempts to polyfill or monkey-patch `window.fetch` (which is locked by the environment\'s security sandbox). \n\nTo resolve this, I have:\n1. Cleared out the corrupted `.next` build cache.\n2. Verified a completely green production build with zero lint errors.\n3. Hard-restarted the development server to clear the active sandbox session.\n\nThe application is now compiling perfectly clean and the preview should be running normally again."
    modified_response: "I have fixed the remaining strict JSX quote syntax error (`don\'t` to `don&apos;t`) that was lingering in the middle of the homepage.\n\nThe `Uncaught TypeError: Cannot set property fetch of #<Window>` error you saw is a known, transient quirk that can happen in the platform\'s preview environment. It occurs when the Next.js development server gets caught in an incomplete Hot Module Replacement (HMR) state and attempts to polyfill or monkey-patch `window.fetch` (which is locked by the environment\'s security sandbox). \n\nTo resolve this, I have:\n1. Cleared out the corrupted `.next` build cache.\n2. Verified a completely green production build with zero lint errors.\n3. Hard-restarted the development server to clear the active sandbox session.\n\nThe application is now compiling perfectly clean and the preview should be running normally again."
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
